{
  "response": "**VERDICT: REJECT**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The `BuggyFileInput` component fails critical accessibility requirements for form labeling and error state communication. While it uses native HTML semantics (which is good), it lacks a programmatic label, does not associate errors with the input field, and fails to announce validation states to assistive technology. Screen reader users will encounter an unlabeled control and will be unaware of validation errors.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**:\n1.  **Missing Label:** The text \"Upload file\" is in a `<div>`, not a `<label>`. Axe confirms this (`label` violation).\n2.  **Error State Blindness:** `setError` updates React state but does not update DOM ARIA attributes (`aria-invalid`) or live regions (`role=\"alert\"`).\n3.  **No Help Text:** File type constraints (`.pdf`, `.docx`) are hidden in the `accept` attribute and invisible to users who don't see the UI placeholder.\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\n\n1.  **File input lacks a visible label associated via `htmlFor`.**\n    - **Evidence:** `src/components/BuggyFileInput.jsx:18-20` renders `<div>Upload file</div>` above the input. The input at line 21 has no `aria-label` and no associated `<label>`. Axe scan confirms `label` violation (critical).\n    - **User Group:** Screen reader users, cognitive accessibility users.\n    - **WCAG/APG:** WCAG 3.3.2 (Labels or Instructions), WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value).\n    - **Fix:** Replace the `<div>Upload file</div>` with a `<label htmlFor=\"file-upload-input\">Upload file</label>` and add `id=\"file-upload-input\"` to the `<input>`.\n\n2.  **Error messages are not associated with the input or announced.**\n    - **Evidence:** `src/components/BuggyFileInput.jsx:23-26` renders errors in a generic `<div className=\"error-message\">`. The input does not have `aria-describedby` pointing to this div, nor does the error div have `role=\"alert\"` or `aria-live=\"assertive\"`.\n    - **User Group:** Screen reader users.\n    - **WCAG/APG:** WCAG 3.3.1 (Error Identification), WCAG 4.1.3 (Status Messages).\n    - **Fix:** Add `id=\"file-error\"` to the error div and `aria-describedby=\"file-error\"` to the input. Add `role=\"alert\"` to the error div so it announces immediately when rendered.\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n\n1.  **Invalid state is not communicated programmatically.**\n    - **Evidence:** `src/components/BuggyFileInput.jsx:10` sets `setError` but does not update the input's `aria-invalid` attribute. The axe census shows the input role is \"button\" with name \"Choose File\", but no invalid state is detected.\n    - **User Group:** Screen reader users.\n    - **WCAG/APG:** WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value).\n    - **Fix:** Add `aria-invalid={!!error}` to the `<input>` element.\n\n2.  **File type constraints are not visible in the label or help text.**\n    - **Evidence:** `src/components/BuggyFileInput.jsx:22` uses `accept=\".pdf,.doc,.docx\"`. This is invisible to users who cannot see the UI (screen readers) and confusing for low-vision users who might miss the visual cue.\n    - **User Group:** Screen reader users, low vision users.\n    - **WCAG/APG:** WCAG 3.3.2 (Labels or Instructions).\n    - **Fix:** Update the label text to include constraints: `<label>Upload file (PDF, DOC, DOCX only)</label>`.\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists):\n\n-   **Error message lacks specific guidance.** The error \"File too large\" is clear but doesn't tell the user *how* to fix it (e.g., \"Please compress your file or choose a smaller one\").\n    - **Fix:** Update error string: `\"File too large. Please choose a file under 5MB.\"`\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases):\n\n-   **Missing `aria-live` region for dynamic errors.** The error appears visually but is silent to screen readers until they tab to the input (and even then, only if `aria-invalid` is present).\n-   **Missing visible help text.** Users relying on cognitive load reduction need to see file constraints *before* attempting to upload.\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n\n-   **Screen reader user:** Hears \"Upload file\" (as a generic text node), then \"Choose File, button\". If an error occurs, the screen reader hears nothing. If they tab back to the input, it announces \"Choose File\", but not that it's invalid or why.\n-   **Keyboard-only user:** Can Tab to the input and click to select a file. No traps. However, if they upload a large file, the error appears visually but provides no focus management (e.g., focusing the input again) to draw attention to the failure.\n-   **Low vision user:** If the error is red text only, it fails WCAG 1.4.1. The label \"Upload file\" might be small or low contrast depending on CSS (not visible in code snippet).\n-   **Cognitive accessibility user:** The separation between the instruction (\"Upload file\") and the constraint (hidden in `accept`) is a cognitive load issue.\n\n**Verdict Justification**:\nThe component fails on two fundamental pillars of form accessibility: labeling and error handling. Without a label, the input is functionally useless to assistive technology. Without proper error association (`aria-describedby`, `role=\"alert\"`, `aria-invalid`), validation feedback is invisible to screen reader users. These are not minor friction points; they are complete access barriers.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n-   Does the parent application provide a global skip link? (Not visible in this snippet).\n-   Is the \"Upload file\" text intended to be the label, or is it decorative? (Assumed label intent based on placement).",
